      en: "Ocoa Bay (Bahía de Ocoa) is located in the Azua province on the southern coast of the Dominican Republic, known for its calm waters and rugged natural beauty. Unlike the humid regions in the east, this area features an arid, semi-desert climate with unique flora, including various cacti and endemic shrubs. The bay stretches for about 15 kilometers, offering numerous pristine beaches with characteristic grey sand and crystal-clear turquoise waters. In recent years, the region has gained fame as a hub for viticulture, with the \"Ocoa Bay\" vineyard pioneering wine production under dry tropical conditions. The bay's waters are rich in marine biodiversity, making it a popular spot for diving and snorkeling among well-preserved coral reefs. Historically, the bay was a strategic site for several naval battles during the Dominican wars of independence. Its relative seclusion has helped maintain its natural charm and protect it from large-scale mass tourism. Environmental initiatives focus on preserving local mangrove forests and ensuring sustainable fishing practices. The sunsets over the bay are widely regarded as some of the most spectacular in the Dominican Republic.",
